<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03R4">Proposition <span data-tag="03R4">4.9.2.8</span> <span class="named">(Cantor's Diagonal Argument)</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a set, and let $P(S)$ denote the collection of all subsets of $S$. Then $|S| &lt; | P(S) |$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The construction $s \mapsto \{ s\} $ determines an injection from $S$ to $P(S)$, which shows that $|S| \leq | P(S) |$. To show that the inequality is strict, it suffices to observe that no function $f: S \rightarrow P(S)$ can be surjective, since the set $T = \{  s \in S: s \notin f(s) \} $ is an element of $P(S)$ which does not belong to the image of $f$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
